# Per-route accessibility baseline — a ratchet, not a floor.
#
# One row per route per theme: `<route> <theme> <error count>`. Tab separated.
# The count is what scripts/pa11y.sh measured when this row was recorded; it is
# not a target and not a claim that the page is clean.
#
# How it is read (scripts/pa11y-report.js):
#
#   * measured > recorded  -> WORSE, the gate fails, both numbers are printed
#   * measured < recorded  -> BETTER, the gate passes and says to re-record
#   * measured = recorded  -> unchanged, the gate passes
#   * row missing here     -> UNTRACKED, reported, does not fail on its own
#   * route missing there  -> SKIPPED (the server refused it) or MISSING (it is
#                             gone from pa11y.config.js), and MISSING fails
#   * `-1` in the count    -> the route was not measured when this was recorded.
#                             Never `0`: a page that cannot be measured must not
#                             look like a page with no errors.
#
# **A duplicated key is a hard failure.** `key -> value` reads as last-one-wins,
# so a second row for the same route and theme silently replaces the number the
# gate compares against, and the gate passes against a value nobody is looking
# at. scripts/pa11y-baseline.js refuses to write one; this reader refuses to
# accept one. The coverage ratchet shipped that bug once.
#
# Re-record deliberately, after reading the diff and agreeing the new number is
# the truth:
#
#     ./scripts/pa11y.sh --record
#
# Regenerating without reading what the run reported is the same as deleting the
# file, with more steps.

